From bb75e1ca408956cf3550f14b65897c7c274f4de9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel <2660793+imDaniel@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:29:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Separate stock graph topology and fuse ordinary-writer
 steering at mHC consume

Replay baseline: r6x author head 7316c7f9fae1bc4a0b7ab1848de0db4143eec5d2 and preceding
0500-series downstream patches. The original base and validation entries
below are historical provenance; r6x qualification is recorded separately.

Base: vLLM 19f2c20ed4d6 with the complete 172-patch r6v author series
and eight downstream steering patches through 0188 (eddd508799a2).
Rationale: route scheduled stock identities before preparing controls. Use
separate FULL graph keys and captures for optional-input topology, with
per-route memory samples; non-stock PIECEWISE executes eagerly. Keep the
stock Tensor/RankOrderParts path and mHC kernel ABI, with no row-control or
projection launch on that route. Only active ordinary writers produce two
compensated FP32 dot components; apply the update in the existing mHC fold.
Retain the legacy nonzero SP projector: reading four rank parts again
would increase large-prefill traffic. Declare both consumer variants before
kernel freeze and retain the existing SP buffer, plus bounded dot storage.
Quality: preserve the FP32 scale identity, API errors, cache namespace,
attention-only scope, rank-order BF16 boundary and compensated formula.
Do not extend the scale range or change weights, precision or capacities.
Validation: 104 CPU contracts pass on macOS and local Linux CPU. Actual
CUDA compilation, numerical replay and throughput require GPU validation;
this local candidate does not claim zero extra active launches or a TPS gain.
Upstream status: independent downstream experiment; not submitted upstream.

Signed-off-by:  <>
